// An interpolated string is parts concatenated, and the pass now lowers it:
// the text between the holes is a `string` const and each hole lowers to the
// value of its own expression, so `interp` answers `string` and the file keeps its
// canonical text. Before this arm the whole literal was one give-up -- 12 over
// the corpus, `expression cannot be lowered to TIR: Interp` -- and the error
// suppressed the file's TIR.
//
// The hole is written \x24{n} because a fixture cannot embed `${`: the lexer
// reading the fixture's own literal would interpolate it. scripts/frontend_gate.py
// decodes that marker in the test plumbing, not as a language escape.
import "../../compiler/tir/entry.orb"

fn main() {
    val source = "fn line(n: int) -> string { return \"count=\x24{n}!\" }"
    print(compileFrontendSource("fixture", source))
}
